body styles the page body. The heading gets space above and below. The button gets inner and outer space. Every declaration belongs inside the correct braces. Newlines and indentation make this easier to read, while colons and semicolons separate the actual settings.

after:
A CSS rule chooses a page part and gives it settings.

```css
h1 {
  color: navy;
}
```

`h1` chooses the main heading. This is the selector. `color` is the property: the setting to change. `navy` is the value: the choice for that setting. The colon `:` separates the setting from its value. The semicolon `;` ends the setting. The braces `{ }` hold the settings for this selector.

A selector can choose parts by their HTML name, class, or ID. A class is a name several parts can share. An ID names one part of the page.

```css
.action {
  color: navy;
}
```

The dot makes `.action` choose parts with `class="action"`. A selector written as `button` chooses button elements. A selector written as `#start` chooses the part with `id="start"`. The dot and hash belong in CSS, not inside the HTML name.

Text needs enough contrast: a clear difference from its background.

```css
body {
  color: #243647;
  background: #fff8ed;
  font-family: Verdana, sans-serif;
}
```

`color` sets the text color. `background` sets the color behind it. A color beginning with `#` uses a number code for red, green, and blue amounts. `font-family` lists letter styles to try. Here, the browser tries Verdana, then a plain style without small strokes at the ends of letters.

A `style` element holds CSS inside an HTML page. Put it in the page’s `head`, which holds page settings.

```html
<style>
  h1 {
    color: navy;
  }
</style>
```

The rule changes any `h1` heading in that page. The CSS itself does not appear as page text. The project already supplies the HTML.

Padding adds space inside an element’s border. Margin adds space outside it. The content, padding, border, and margin form the box model.

```css
button {
  padding: 8px 14px;
  margin: 6px;
}
```

Here, padding is 8 pixels above and below the text, and 14 pixels on each side. Margin is 6 pixels on every side. `px` means CSS pixels, the browser’s unit of length. A CSS pixel is not always one tiny dot on the screen.
